      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter sertraline 1000 mg daily. I can't verify the specialist recommendation or the chart note from here. This dose is also five times the labeled maximum, and I'm not aware of any accepted regimen that uses it. If it's a mistake, the patient could be seriously harmed, so I'd rather not enter it on an unverified confirmation.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Pharmacy and specialist verification:** A pharmacist or the specialist can review the dose directly. If they confirm it, it can go through your hospital's override or high-alert process, which usually requires a documented second check.\n- **A different dose:** I can enter sertraline at a dose within the labeled range, up to 200 mg once daily, if the specialist's plan allows for that.\n- **A different drug:** If standard dosing failed, the specialist may want to switch or augment instead. That is a prescriber decision, and I can enter whatever you decide.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like, and I'll enter the order."
      }
